questions.scenario:
You are debugging a production issue where an audit log service fails to record entries during payment processing under load. You inspect the following Spring Boot service snippet:

java
@Service
public class PaymentAuditService {

    @Autowired
    private AuditRepository auditRepository;

    @Transactional
    public CompletableFuture<Void> recordAuditAsync(PaymentEvent event) {
        return CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
            auditRepository.save(new AuditLog(event));
            if (event.isFailed()) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Payment validation failed");
            }
        });
    }
}


Callers invoke `recordAuditAsync(event)` inside an outer `@Transactional` business workflow. In production, when `IllegalStateException` is thrown, the outer payment transaction commits successfully anyway, while partial audit logs are intermittently committed or dropped without trace.

You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput microservice migrated to Java 21 and Spring Boot 3.2 with Virtual Threads enabled (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`). During a peak flash-sale event, the microservice experiences severe latency spikes, CPU degradation, and carrier thread starvation. Thread dumps reveal thousands of virtual threads in the `WAITING` state, but the underlying carrier thread pool (`ForkJoinPool`) has no available workers to mount runnable virtual threads.

You isolate the following legacy hashing and caching module:

java
public class SessionCache {
    private final Map<String, SessionData> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public synchronized SessionData getOrCreate(String sessionId, Supplier<SessionData> loader) {
        SessionData data = cache.get(sessionId);
        if (data == null) {
            data = loader.get(); // Performs a blocking HTTPS RPC call to external IAM
            cache.put(sessionId, data);
        }
        return data;
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where an order orchestration service deadlocks under sustained high load. The service uses a custom bounded `ThreadPoolExecutor` to handle concurrent enrichment steps:

java
ExecutorService executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
    8, 8, 0L, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS,
    new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000)
);

public CompletableFuture<OrderDTO> enrichOrder(Order order) {
    return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
        CompletableFuture<CustomerInfo> customerFuture = 
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchCustomer(order.getCustomerId()), executor);
        CompletableFuture<InventoryInfo> inventoryFuture = 
            CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchInventory(order.getItemId()), executor);

        // Wait for both sub-tasks to complete
        return new OrderDTO(order, customerFuture.join(), inventoryFuture.join());
    }, executor);
}


At low traffic, the endpoint returns in 50ms. Under sustained load of 50 RPS, active threads reach 8 and all incoming requests time out indefinitely.
